\chapter{Mu3AC: making evaluation order explicit}\label{ch:mu3ac}
\begin{MuObjectives}
\item Read a three-address operation as a result and at most two value inputs.
\item Follow control flow, lexical cells, and call parameters separately.
\item Explain why a dynamic language backend still needs runtime services.
\end{MuObjectives}

MuLLIER is a tree representation well suited to syntax-level analysis and
equality saturation. A native backend also needs an explicit sequence: where
values are produced, which branch executes next, when an environment is entered,
and how arguments reach a call. Mu3AC supplies that representation without
replacing MuLL's dynamic value model with an unrelated integer language.

\section{A public compile-only boundary}
The two hosts now expose \api{Runtime.parse(source,name)} and
\api{Runtime.compile(source,name)}. The first returns the original tree in
MuLLIER's interchange format. The second applies the existing semantic policies
and optional optimization, using the runtime's known global mutability. Neither
evaluates user code or imports a package. Existing \api{run} behavior remains
the default interpreter path.
\begin{cppcode}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include "mull/mu3ac/Mu3AC.hpp"
mull::Runtime runtime;
auto ir = runtime.compile("return 6*7;", "answer.mull");
auto program = mull::mu3ac::Lowering{}.compile(ir);
mull::mu3ac::Rewriter rewrite;
rewrite.optimize(program);
auto assembly = mull::mu3ac::X86_64{}.emit(program);
\end{cppcode}
The D surface has the same stages:
\begin{dcode}
import MuDSL;
import mull.mu3ac.Mu3AC;
auto runtime = new MuLL.Runtime;
auto program = (new Lowering).compile(
    runtime.compile("return 6*7;", "answer.mull"));
(new Rewriter).optimize(program);
auto assembly = (new X86_64).emit(program);
\end{dcode}
Assembly emission does not itself invoke a toolchain. The CLI owns assembly,
linking, output paths, and process execution.

\section{Registers are values, not bindings}
A \api{Program} contains functions. Each function has parameter names, a virtual
register count, and an instruction sequence. Arithmetic and indexing consume at
most two value registers and produce one. Registers contain opaque runtime
handles, not raw host objects. They are non-SSA: explicit moves assign results
at branch joins, and registers begin as nil.

Lexical names refer to environment cells, separately from these temporaries.
\api{load} snapshots a cell's current value into a register. \api{bind}
creates a binding with its mutability; \api{store} updates a mutable cell;
\api{alias} makes a reference to an existing mutable cell. \api{enter} and
\api{leave} express scope transitions. Closures retain the live environment,
not a copied table of values, preserving late declarations and captured writes.

\begin{mullcode}
var total <- 0;
val add <- fn(x) { total <- total + x; return total; };
add(10);
return add(5);
\end{mullcode}
The calls above return through compiled function bodies in the native backend.
Their shared \api{total} is still one lexical cell.

\section{Control flow replaces implicit tree traversal}
\api{label}, \api{jump}, \api{branch-false}, and \api{return} make sequencing
visible. \api{and}/\api{or} become branches and moves, so the unused operand
does not execute and the result remains an operand value rather than a coerced
Boolean. Zero stays truthy.

\begin{mullcode}
var count <- 0;
while count < 8 {
  count <- count + 1;
  { if count > 2 { break; } }
}
return count;
\end{mullcode}
A break leaves exactly the active loop-body scopes before jumping to the exit.
The result of a while statement is updated only after a complete iteration;
breaking partway through the next iteration does not replace that result.
Function returns jump to their own epilogue and cannot become loop breaks.

\api{check-store} is deliberately separate from \api{store}: assignment checks
that its target exists and is mutable \emph{before} evaluating its RHS. Reversing
these operations could print output or call a host function before reporting an
immutable-target error, changing the language's effect ordering.

\section{Arguments, collections, and source sites}
The callee is evaluated first, then argument expressions from left to right.
Adjacent \api{param} instructions transfer the resulting handles to a bounded
call scratch area; \api{call} consumes them. \api{list} and \api{record} use the
same explicit parameter mechanism, with separate record-key metadata.
\api{member}, \api{index}, and \api{closure} complete the current value surface.
Host-backed MuLIB values keep their existing member/index/call contracts.

Every instruction retains a source line and byte column. \api{tick} marks
evaluated source-node work; it is not an arbitrary machine-instruction counter.
Native helpers restore the appropriate site before operations that can fail.
Thus a division-by-zero or callback error can still identify a MuLL location.

\section{An inspection format with explicit bounds}
\api{Program.dump()} emits the same version-1 S-expression format in C++ and D.
The following is schematic; the exact register numbering depends on lowering:
\begin{rulecode}
(mu3ac 1
  (function 0 2 (params)
    (tick (site 1 1))
    (number 1 "42" (site 1 8))
    (return 1 (site 1 1))))
\end{rulecode}
The current CLI writes this format for inspection, not as a second source input.
Lowering bounds functions at 4096, registers per function at 4096, total
instructions at 262144, and arguments, parameters, or literal collection entries
at 256. These are compiler representation limits, not the size limit of a list
returned by a library. Unsupported or oversized lowering fails explicitly;
there is no hidden fallback that interprets the entry source instead.

\begin{MuSummary}
\item Temporary value registers and persistent lexical cells solve different problems.
\item Branches and explicit parameter transfer preserve MuLL's evaluation order.
\item Source sites and limits are part of the backend contract, not afterthoughts.
\end{MuSummary}
